Original commit body from 08943ac01d22fa1b764190266e5abce72629e2d1:

The WI-kosup knob census found catalogue loaders that silently ignore an
unknown key -- the file reports loaded and the key does nothing. Taint
(WI-rinaz) and io_primitives (PR #1927) refuse them; this does the
frameworks, library_signatures, function_summaries and dataflow_patterns
loaders, through the same yaml_catalogs.reject_unknown_keys, naming the
file, the level and the key, with a near-miss hint.

Levels checked:
- frameworks: top level, each pattern, each pattern's usage: (wrapped
  into the existing CatalogueError naming the file);
- library_signatures: top level (a `signature:` typo read as no rows);
- function_summaries: top level, each entry, callback:, sanitizes.<i>;
- dataflow_patterns (shipped load): top level and library_patterns rows.
  The user channel still reads library_patterns only and ignores the
  rest, by its documented, test-pinned decision; unchanged.

Accepted-but-unread keys, deleted:
- frameworks linkers: (101 files). Read into FrameworkPatternDef.linkers
  and by nothing outside tests; a linker's activation is its
  LinkerActivation in code. The field is gone, and so is the
  ARCHITECTURE.md generator line telling authors to add the section.
  Four tests asserting "framework X activates linker Y" asserted
  something that was never true and are deleted.
- frameworks detect: (zio, swiftui, hummingbird): no reader.
- library_signatures module_completeness: partial (go, java, objc,
  python, swift): no reader; three test fixtures dropped it too.
- dataflow calls: / DataflowConfig.calls: produced by no file; the field,
  its two map loops and its two tests are gone (a calls: section is now
  refused).

Rules that matched nothing, deleted with a note in the file:
- graphql-python.yaml's Graphene resolver rule (name_pattern: ^resolve_,
  unread). The supported spelling (method_name + parent_base_class)
  cannot match either: only java.py and objc.py stamp
  parent_base_classes, so a python method never carries it. Checked on a
  fixture: Query(graphene.ObjectType).resolve_hello gets no concept
  before or after.
- zio.yaml's two zio-http route rules (usage_call:, unread). The scala
  analyzer emits no usage context for Routes(Method.GET / "x" ->
  handler(...)), so a usage: kind call rule would match nothing either
  (fixture checked).
Both gaps, and the same parent_base_classes gap in flask-restful's
route_handler rule, are filed as WI-sorop.

Every shipped framework, library-signature, function-summary and
dataflow file loads (pinned by test). The strictness found one more live
case, in a test: test_user_catalogue_channels' in-house framework fixture
wrote name: / symbol_patterns: / decorators: -- none of them keys -- so it
loaded zero patterns while asserting only 'not None'; it now writes
patterns: / decorator: and asserts the route rule loads. A summary file
claiming user_supplied: is now refused outright rather than ignored.
docs/CONCEPTS.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md regenerated. Not done here: function-summary
VALUES that no consumer reads (census K7) and dataflow row values the
maps ignore (K13's returns[]._default etc.) -- outside this item's text.

Tests: test_catalogue_unknown_keys.py -- a refused key per loader and
level, every shipped file in each family loads, no linkers field, no
shipped module_completeness. Mutation-checked: disabling each loader's
top-level/entry check reds its test (4 of 4).

